# The park as a whole: the card that the park's other cards name as their parent (id `park_scene`).
from grass_lib import *
W_GP = X('Greenwich Park (Wikipedia)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenwich_Park')
N('park_scene', 'Greenwich Park', 'habitat', 300, [],
  "The park in this explorer: a small, simplified patch of Greenwich Park in London, with a person, a dog, oaks, a pond, a boulder, a robin, a bee, a mushroom, buttercups and a lawn, drawn at true size under the real Sun for the chosen date and time.",
  "You start in the park. Everything standing in it is a model drawn at its real size, except the lawn, which is a stand-in, so the bee is 14 mm long next to a dog and a person. Click any living thing, or the ground, the pond, the Sun or the sky, to open a card about it. Scroll toward something to go inside it: through the skin and muscle of the dog or yourself, into a leaf of the oak or the grass, down through the pond water or the soil, and on down to cells and molecules. The lighting comes from the real Sun for the date and time shown on the card, so the park is lit differently in June and December.",
  "The real Greenwich Park is a Royal Park in the Royal Borough of Greenwich, London, of about 74 hectares. The Royal Observatory stands on the hill in the middle of the park, and the historic Prime Meridian, Airy's meridian marked by a line at the Observatory, crosses the park. The zero line of longitude used by satellite navigation lies about 102 metres east of that line, because the old line was fixed by the local vertical at the telescope; the park straddles both. The park is part of the Maritime Greenwich World Heritage Site. Its southern half is a gravel plateau up to about 45 m above sea level, and its northern part is low-lying flood plain. The park in this explorer is not a map of it: the ground is a simplified disc, and what stands on it (the pond, the oaks, the boulders, the lawn) are models placed to show the kinds of living thing and material you can meet in a British park.",
  "Every object in the park is a model built from typical measurements and says so on its own card; nothing here is a scan of a real tree, animal or pond. The oaks, the grass and the buttercups are drawn after the species named on their cards; positions and numbers are choices.",
  [('Real park, area', '≈ 74 ha'), ('Real park, position', 'the Royal Observatory and the historic Prime Meridian are in the park; the satellite-navigation zero meridian is ≈ 102 m east of the Observatory line'), ('Status', 'Royal Park; part of the Maritime Greenwich World Heritage Site'), ('In this explorer', 'a simplified patch with true-size models (the lawn is a stand-in), lit by the real Sun for the chosen date and time')],
  'C1', [], [W_GP, PARKSGARD, MALYS, X('Greenwich Park (The Royal Parks)', 'https://www.royalparks.org.uk/visit/parks/greenwich-park')], parts=['human_body', 'dog', 'oak_english', 'park_bee', 'rn_acris', 'park_mushroom', 'robin', 'park_pond', 'boulder', 'grass_plant', 'grass_lawn', 'park_ground', 'park_sky', 'park_sun', 'park_cloud'], related=['park_ground', 'grass_lawn'])
